<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  What DunneFlow asks the hardened runtime for, and today the answer is nothing.

  An entitlement is a claim that the ordinary rules have to be relaxed for this program, and
  every PyInstaller guide on the internet carries the same four of them, copied from each
  other. None of the four is indicated here, and each was checked rather than assumed:

  * com.apple.security.automation.apple-events — the folder picker spawns /usr/bin/osascript,
    Apple's own signed binary, running `activate` and `choose folder`. Those are Standard
    Additions commands that execute inside osascript itself; no Apple Event is sent to another
    application, and the entitlement that would be needed belongs to that process rather than
    to this one. See web/serve.py, _p_pickdir.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit and com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    — CPython 3.12 has no JIT, and nothing here builds a ctypes callback closure. That second
    clause is now load bearing rather than incidental: dunneflow/macapp.py calls into AppKit
    through ctypes so the application appears in the Dock with a running indicator, and it
    does so entirely by calling functions that already exist. It installs no
    NSApplicationDelegate, because defining an Objective C class with Python methods is
    exactly the closure this claim rules out; quit is NSApplication's own default handling of
    the quit Apple Event instead. tests/test_frozen.py asserts the module contains no
    CFUNCTYPE, so the claim above cannot quietly stop being true. Pressed rather than
    assumed: the Developer ID signed build, hardened runtime on and this dictionary empty,
    registers with the window server, serves, and quits cleanly, with nothing denied in the
    AMFI log.

  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — every Mach-O file in the bundle is
    signed by the same Developer ID that signs the bundle, so library validation has nothing
    to refuse. This one would come back the moment a dependency shipped a dylib signed by
    somebody else.

  So the dictionary is empty, deliberately, and the file is here rather than absent because
  release.sh names it and because an empty list of claims is itself a claim worth reading.

  To add one: put the key in, and put the failure that earned it in the comment above it —
  what was run, what the system said, and where. An entitlement with no reproduced failure
  beside it is a guess wearing a uniform.

  Note for whoever edits this: AMFI parses the file, and its XML is stricter than most. A
  comment before the DOCTYPE is a syntax error, and so is a double hyphen anywhere inside a
  comment. Both were paid for here.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
